+++ w/src/postgres/src/include/catalog/pg_proc.dat
@@ -4070,9 +4070,9 @@
   proname => 'yb_servers', proretset => 't', provolatile => 'v',
   prorettype => 'record',
   proargtypes => '',
-  proallargtypes => '{text,int8,int8,text,text,text,text,text,text}',
-  proargmodes => '{o,o,o,o,o,o,o,o,o}',
-  proargnames => '{host,port,num_connections,node_type,cloud,region,zone,public_ip,uuid}',
+  proallargtypes => '{text,int8,int8,text,text,text,text,text,text,jsonb}',
+  proargmodes => '{o,o,o,o,o,o,o,o,o,o}',
+  proargnames => '{host,port,num_connections,node_type,cloud,region,zone,public_ip,uuid,jsonb}',
   prosrc => 'yb_servers' },
 
 { oid => '2627',
```

though it seems to be buggy on selecting "zone" specifically ("cloud" and "region" work), probably some fault in my implementation:

```
yugabyte=# select jsonb from yb_servers();
                             jsonb                             
---------------------------------------------------------------
 {"cloud": "cloud1", "region": "datacenter1", "zone": "rack1"}
(1 row)

yugabyte=# select jsonb->'zone' from yb_servers();
 ?column? 
----------
 
(1 row)
```

As for json or jsonb, I'm not sure which would be better.  The functions likely won't be querying jsonb data stored on disk, so those performance benefits probably don't apply.  I think it comes down to which is easier to implement.  For json, we need to be extra careful to construct the json correctly in the first place, so I think jsonb with a universal converter is an easier approach.

See related issues #11906, #13797.
